1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,693
Your CPU might say 8 cores and 16 threads.

2
00:00:03,973 --> 00:00:06,439
So what's a thread, and why isn't it a core?

3
00:00:07,189 --> 00:00:09,521
A thread is one stream of instructions.

4
00:00:09,801 --> 00:00:16,553
A core is the hardware that runs it: units that do the math, plus a record of where the thread is up to.

5
00:00:17,303 --> 00:00:20,862
Cores often sit idle, waiting for data from memory.

6
00:00:21,142 --> 00:00:24,001
Hyper-Threading lets one core hold two threads.

7
00:00:24,281 --> 00:00:27,550
When one stalls, the other uses the idle units.

8
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:30,437
But both threads share the same units.

9
00:00:30,717 --> 00:00:36,134
That's why Intel's first version promised up to thirty percent more speed, not double.

10
00:00:36,884 --> 00:00:38,062
Some chips skip it.

11
00:00:38,342 --> 00:00:47,958
Intel's Core Ultra 9 285K has 24 cores and just 24 threads, because Hyper-Threading was removed.

12
00:00:48,708 --> 00:00:53,558
How one core runs your code is in our video, How a CPU Runs Your Code.

